{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787424890,"nonce":"Gc6gAUaLnJb6h0U2deXLS/L5Nag=","title":"1. **The cost is per-REQUEST, not per-byte.** The cold drain moved 8.19 MB in 5360 ms — 1.7 MB/s over *loopback*, absurd as bandwidth and exactly right as 2000 requests at ~2.4 ms each. Optimise request count; do not optimise throughput.","message":"2. **Wire batching is NOT storage packing.** Independent. Batching changes only the HTTP surface: reversible, no migration, no format change. Storage packing (`a0c28aa`) changes the frozen on-disk canonical form and needs its own ADR. Do not let a reviewer conflate them — this one can land now.\n3. **The integrity boundary does not move.** The Depot re-hashes every member and refuses a mismatch. Without that, a client could store bytes under an address it does not own. Batching makes the *envelope* bigger; it must not make verification coarser — verify EACH member independently and report per-member.\n4. **Content addressing makes batching safe.** Every member is idempotent, so any subset can be retried and a re-send is a no-op. There is no partial-batch corruption state to design around — only a partial-batch *report*.\n5. **Measure with client and Depot isolated.** The 2026-08-02 sweep saturated at 4x (1-\u003e1800 ms, 4-\u003e434, 16-\u003e434, 128-\u003e431) with both in ONE process sharing ten cores. That saturation is probably a harness artefact. Re-sweep isolated BEFORE concluding anything about concurrency width.\n6. **A fence token must not be an unbounded upload credential.** Same discipline as `/v1/drains`: bound members and bytes per batch, explicitly, in the route.\n7. **There is a syscall floor the wire cannot go below.** Only 434 ms of the feed's 1076 ms is transfer; 590 ms (55%) is local writes + metadata restore. Batching cannot touch that half. State the floor rather than promising past it.\n8. **`/have` already proves the shape.** The batched existence question exists and works; this completes a half-built pattern rather than introducing a new one.\n\n\n**Upload — `POST /v1/cas/blobs:batch`**\n\nStreamed frames, no count header (read to EOF):\n\n    [32 bytes raw sha256][u64 len big-endian][len bytes] ...repeat...\n\nResponse is **per-member**, because one bad member must not fail the batch ambiguously:\n\n    200 { \"accepted\": [\"\u003chex\u003e\", ...], \"rejected\": [ {\"hash\":\"\u003chex\u003e\", \"reason\":\"digest mismatch\"} ] }\n\n- Auth: unchanged — any valid workspace token carrying a fence claim.\n- Frame budget: seal at whichever comes first, **8 MiB or 256 members**. Bounds memory on both sides, keeps a retry cheap, stays under typical proxy body limits.\n- Frames go N-wide concurrently, exactly as single PUTs do today.\n- **Trees use the same route and STILL ledger per accepted member** — the ledger append must remain per-tree, never per-batch, or a batch containing one foreign hash would launder it. This is the `e58ce1f` exfiltration fix; do not weaken it.\n\n**Fetch — `POST /v1/cas/blobs:fetch`**\n\nRequest: newline-delimited hex hashes. Response: same framing plus a per-member status, so a missing blob is *reported* rather than silently omitted:\n\n    [32 bytes hash][u8 status][u64 len][len bytes]     status 0 = ok\n    [32 bytes hash][u8 status]                          status 1 = not found\n\nPairs naturally with `/v1/cas/trees/{root}/flat`, which already returns the whole manifest in one call: the client fetches the manifest, diffs it against what it holds, and requests the missing set in framed batches.\n\n\n- [ ] **Re-measure with client and Depot in separate processes** (principle 5) and re-sweep concurrency width. Do this FIRST or we tune a harness\n- [ ] `blobs:batch` upload + `blobs:fetch`, per the framing above, with per-member results and explicit bounds\n- [ ] Client uses them in `wsfetch fetch` and `wsfetch drain`; single-object routes stay for compatibility and for the skew window\n- [ ] **Parallelise tree PUTs** — currently a serial `for` loop while blobs are 16-wide. Free win, same slice\n- [ ] Make `CONCURRENCY` (hard-coded 16 at `crates/scarab-workspace-client/src/lib.rs:64`) configurable, informed by the re-sweep — note it is NOT `SCARAB_CAS_CONCURRENCY`, which is the Depot's own object-store legs\n- [ ] Check what `reqwest`/`axum` actually negotiate: 16 HTTP/1.1 connections means 16 handshakes and 16 congestion windows; **h2** multiplexes over one. May be a config-only win\n- [ ] Re-measure and record in the same shape as git-bug `4ce7f2c` so the numbers stay comparable\n\n\n- **Storage packing** — `a0c28aa`, separate ADR, frozen-format change.\n- **zstd on the wire** — a *loss* on loopback and a win over a real network, so it must be negotiated, and our loopback harness will systematically under-value it. Revisit with a realistic-RTT measurement.\n- **The feed's filesystem half** (590 of 1076 ms) — parallel writes, `fchmod`/`futimens` on the open fd instead of re-resolving the path, folding the `widen_for_the_group` chmod walk into the write, possibly `io_uring`. Its own ticket; no wire change helps it.\n- **blake3** — but note the honest connection: every byte is hashed TWICE (client computes the address, Depot verifies). That is trigger #1 in ADR-0066 point 11, and it has nothing to do with laziness. If batching lands and hashing becomes the visible cost, that is the measurement that tags blake3 in.\n\n\nBatching plus parallel tree PUTs should take the drain from ~2.7 ms/file to well under 1. At that point hashing and syscalls become the visible costs, and the next decision is blake3 versus `io_uring` — not more wire work.","files":null}]}